Data Structures Overview

 Data Structure
 Stores
 Keeps Order?
 Allows Duplicates?
 Best For

 ArrayList
 Ordered elements
 Yes
 Yes
 Accessing elements by index

 HashSet
 Unique elements
 No
 No
 Avoiding duplicates, fast checks

 HashMap
 Key-value pairs
 No
 Yes (keys are unique)
 Fast lookup by key

Queue

 A Queue follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle. In Java, you can implement a queue using the Queue interface, often with a LinkedList.

 Example: Using Queue
 import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class ExampleQueue {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 // Create a Queue of Strings using LinkedList
 Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
 queue.add("First");
 queue.add("Second");
 queue.add("Third");

 // Process the queue: remove and print each element
 while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
 System.out.println(queue.poll());
 }
 }
}

 Try it Yourself »

 Here, the Queue is implemented using a LinkedList. The poll() method retrieves and removes the head of the queue.

Stack

